4. ChatGPR INFO

That error happens because you’re running the container as UID 1003 (via user: "${UID}:${DOCKER_GID}"), 
but there’s no /etc/passwd entry for UID 1003 inside the container. Some programs (like lftp) 
call getpwuid() and fail hard if the UID isn’t a known user, even if HOME is set. Hence: “No user exists for uid 1003.”


5. Build-time user (most “proper,” requires rebuild)
Refer to current Dockerfile.runtime

6. Build it with your host IDs

# Get your host IDs
id -u         # -> e.g., 1003
getent group docker | cut -d: -f3   # -> e.g., 995

# Build (Compose will also do this if you run `docker compose build app_tools`)
docker compose build \
  --build-arg APP_UID="$(id -u)" \
  --build-arg APP_GID="$(getent group docker | cut -d: -f3)" \
  app_tools



Notes / gotchas
This image is now tied to the UID/GID provided at build time. If a teammate with different IDs builds it, they should pass their own APP_UID/APP_GID.
Bind mounts still honor host-side permissions. Make sure your target dirs on the host allow group docker to write (one-time):

This setup removes the need for entrypoint hacks, satisfies lftp’s user lookup, and ensures files the job creates are yourUID:docker with predictable permissions.

How you’ve fixed it (build-time user)
By baking a user into the image with:
APP_UID=$(id -u) → 1003
APP_GID=$(getent group docker | cut -d: -f3) → your docker group GID
…you add an /etc/passwd entry for UID 1003 in the image. Now getpwuid(1003) succeeds and lftp stops complaining.